WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.100
Alert design begins with a defined reviewer and response.

2
00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:09.080
Route events by location, schedule, type, and severity.

3
00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:17.980
Immediate notification should be reserved for events with a legitimate time-sensitive action, while lower-priority candidates enter a review queue.

4
00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:22.740
Across multiple cameras, one event may appear in several feeds.

5
00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:29.800
Correlation and grouping should combine related detections into a single case with a timeline and relevant views.

6
00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:34.380
Cooldowns prevent an ongoing condition from generating repeated notifications.

7
00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:40.820
Confidence thresholds, zones, object size, duration, and schedules can reduce noise.

8
00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.840
They do not remove uncertainty.

9
00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:49.580
When evidence is weak, the interface should mark the event for ordinary review rather than escalate it as a confirmed incident.

10
00:00:49.860 --> 00:00:57.300
Human reviewers need the event clip, neighboring time, camera identity, model output, and available context.

11
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:03.160
They should be able to confirm, dismiss, defer, annotate, and escalate.

12
00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.100
High-consequence action should not rely on an unreviewed model label.

13
00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:09.300
Document decisions and outcomes.

14
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:18.920
Feedback can identify duplicate cameras, harmless recurring motion, poor placement, model drift, or an escalation rule that no longer fits operations.

15
00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:23.020
Protect review queues and notes with role-based access.

16
00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:30.260
Measure relevance, missed events, duplicate rate, time to review, escalation outcome, and reviewer workload.

17
00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:34.920
Alert fatigue is an architecture failure, not a reviewer weakness.

18
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:40.220
A sustainable system sends fewer, better-supported events to people who know what to do with them.
